%U A400559 2567540840087097665089,454936357149014623450400,87075475844348116209905325,17903853288234982738335138496
%N A400559 Number of triples (B, g, e) with B a strongly connected digraph on n labeled nodes, g and e nodes of B, such that adding a new node r with edges e -> r and r -> g yields a digraph reducible as a flow graph rooted at every node.
%C A400559 The single-entry-single-exit (SESE) blocks of everywhere-reducible strong digraphs: every block of the region decomposition of such a digraph is one of these, and the everywhere-reducible count is the plain species composition Flat(C(x)) of this sequence's e.g.f. C(x) into the flat skeleton (see labeled_strong_all_valid_headers in the linked program of A400123).
%C A400559 The closed form below (via C = x*Phi(C), Phi the flat_plus_one_return e.g.f., closed by the return-attachment theorem) extends the sequence to all orders.
%D A400559 M. S. Hecht and J. D. Ullman, Flow graph reducibility, SIAM J. Comput. 1 (1972), 188-202.
%D A400559 M. S. Hecht and J. D. Ullman, Characterizations of reducible flow graphs, J. ACM 21 (1974), 367-375.
%D A400559 R. W. Robinson, Counting digraphs with restrictions on the strong components, in T.-H. Ku (ed.), Combinatorics and Graph Theory '95, World Scientific, Singapore, 1995, pp. 343-354.
%D A400559 R. E. Tarjan, Testing flow graph reducibility, J. Comput. System Sci. 9 (1974), 355-365.
%F A400559 Let C(x) = Sum_{n>=1} a(n)*x^n/n!. Then C(x) = x*Phi(C(x)), where Phi(z) = exp(tau(z)) + tau(z)*exp(tau(z))*(2*exp(tau(z))-1)/(2*exp(tau(z))-1-2*tau(z)*exp(tau(z))) and tau(z) = z*(2*exp(tau(z))-1)*(2*exp(tau(z))-1-tau(z)).
%F A400559 Thus a(n) = n!*[x^n] C(x).
%o A400559 (Python) everywhere_reducible_sequence("sese_all_valid_blocks", 20) # See Python link in A400123 for program code.
%Y A400559 Cf. A003030, A052873.
